  "id": "lateral-lunge",
  "name": "Lateral Lunge",
  "definition": "A bodyweight side-step lunge in which one foot travels laterally by 60 percent of body height, the lead leg descends to a self-selected depth, and the athlete drives back to bilateral standing. Hip abduction and adduction and the corresponding gluteus-medius and adductor-longus/brevis roles are transparent anatomy-and-mechanics inferences from the source-exact lateral displacement and Arnold capability map; Riemann et al. directly measured sagittal lower-limb mechanics but did not measure frontal-plane joint moments, muscle activity, or a relative muscle ranking. Forward, reverse, walking, Cossack, split-squat, lateral step-up, jumping, loaded, and differently standardized stride variants remain outside this family.",
  "evidenceRefs": ["arnold-2010-lower-limb", "riemann-2013-forward-lateral-lunge"],
